Turkey Is Expanding Diplomatic Horizons
Long before Turkey tried to enter the European Union, the European powers were anxious to penetrate deep into the Turkish hinterland.
On the eastern part of the Bosphorus, perhaps 20 steps in Asia, there is one of the most beautiful relics of this failed imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha Train Station was designed by two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu neo-Renaissance.
That’s at least what tour guides tell you.
The ecstatishes between the ends of the platforms with a tower on each of its corners looking to the world as some German provincial municipalities.
The stone's dirty. - Yeah.
Entirely decadent eigenque.
“Orkiously inside the vaulted ceiling ticket office, there’s no doubt about the tensivity of the hurdle-de-tax enterprise of what was the passage further west.
The station was opened in August 1908 by a German consortium, Anatolia-Baghdad Corporation.
Germany, a latarian at the imperial gamble, wished to realize its ambitions to dominate the Middle East by means of this railway that ran in Damascus, Baghdad and beyond what is now Saudi Arabia.
But Germany’s imperial ambitiations sank during the First World War.
Its railroads were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and its irregular Arabs.
Its isolation from the surrounding region, generated by the icy borders of the Cold War
British and dominatrix forces advanced out of Egypt and propelled the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
And there was bad news for Hyderpasha station too.
In 1917 a munitions-excruciating train destroyed its elaborate roof.
Turkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then it’s slowly unrattled, vastly surpassed by history.
But nowadays, this railway outlet for Anatolia and all the points in the east, it is necessary - at least symbolically - to highlight the ever-greater diplomatic horizons of Turkey.
The ruling Justice-and-Development Party – the AKP—has created an all-new foreign policy for the country.
Relative insusisolation from its surrounding regions, spawned on the icy borders of the Cold War has passed.
Now there's a new policy of commitment.
Remarkably the Turkish government has good relations with Israel and Syria, with the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran and of course with the European Union and the United States.
To estimating the metrics of this new foreign policy, I embarked on the sleeping car for the Turkish capital Ankara to visit one of AKP’s leading foreign policy experts, Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning—about ten-and-a-half hours later—after a snuggle-drops along the slopes that produced ancient world cadences of the famous documentary The Night Mail, I arrived in the Turkish capital – proposed as a new city for a centuries-old state intent on turning their backs on its Ottoman past.
But the AKP with its well-known Islamic roots represents an exemplary shift in Turkish consent.
He has suggested some suspects in the West, most notably in Washington.
A taxi takes me to the AKP headquarters, an ultra-modern structure of stone and steel - built ad hoc - in a developing suburb, surrounded by shopping malls half built and confectioned confectionery of power, where managers in complete businesses - both men and women - meet to make contracts early in the morning.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a center-right politician from almost every other political party in Europe.
Complete, tie, I'm sure he had a Blackberry as well.
"The aim of the foreign policy party now is to replenish Turkey with its nearest neighbours.
“Cundal war was an anomaly,” he tells me.
Turkey has been cut off from its hinterland.
Today it must look east and west at the same time.
That wasn't the case with one or the other.
It has to be both.
“Would you definitely at a certain point, ‘I’m asking, ‘Will Turkey be compelled to decide?’
"Turkey," he says, "doesn't want where to face that choice."
Then it underlines a point that almost everyone with whom I have spoken here recited by heart: the ties of not only with the Middle East, but also with the Caucasus, and all around the Black Sea, are as beneficial to Europe and the United States as they are for Turkey.
With Turkey on board, the message appears to be, the whole of Europe can metaphorically travel those railway lines east across Asia and in the Middle East.
Back at Hydarpasha, after another blank night, I'm thinking of those two German gentlemen Herr Ritter and Herr Conu who had looked at this building built 100 years ago.
What they would have been surprised to find that Europe’s Middle-Eastern ambitions can now be, in large part, in the hands of Turkey.
And as I'm waiting at the terminal for the ferry that takes me back through the Bosphorus, I remember that those days Turkey has always been called "the sick man of Europe".
At the time, at least there seemed to be no doubt as to what a continent were supposed to make claims on this fascinating country.
