- William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
Your obituary by William Woodruff (Sept. (June 25)) specifies that "in 1946 he resumed studying economic history at Oxford and in 1950 became a researcher in Houblon-Norman ... studying at the same time for a bachelor's degree in science, one in letters and a PhD."
This results from Woodruff's author's note in Beyond Nab End, but it's incorrect.
From 1946 to 1953 he lectured in economic history at the University of Nottingham, at which time he completed a Ph.D. thesis on the British Rubber Industry, supervised by Professor J. D. Chambers, and later published in 1958 as The British Rubber Industry.
He spent 1950–51 in the U.S. as a researcher at Houblon-Norman, and on the summer of 1952 at the Universities of Illinois, Harvard, and New York.
In 1953 he left Nottingham permanently for Illinois.
“Wearruff chose to cover this part of his career, I don’t know.
