The Second Assault on Honda Supersales
In November the Honda will begin the commercialization of the second-th-moonment of the Jazz model.
If the previous model was already endowed with great habitability, this new minivan has an even larger interior space.
A refined mechanic must be added, which increases the driving comfort and saves fuel.
An evolution against a revolution.
That's the second-th-moon of the Honda Jazz.
Albeit aesthetically the new Jazz has a line very similar to that of the previous model, the changes in this minivan are numerous.
Among others, it is worth mentioning an innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new mechanics.
However, before switching to analysing the new model, it’s worth pointing out that this new Jazz arrives six years after entering the first-generation market.
Therefore, an initial retaliation is that Honda has refurbished this model in a shorter time than those used by other manufacturers.
Although in Europe the Jazz model is less well-received than in other markets, it remains a unique phenomenon in terms of sales.
That’s exactly the case in Japan where Jazz was the best-gross-sold car in 2002 and also during this race 2008.
Precisely in Japan almost a million Jazz units have been sold since its launch.
The second market has been that of the Old Continent, with about half a million units in six years.
In spite of these numbers, Honda believes that the new Jazz will increase sales in the segment.
Therefore, if in 2007 the Japanese company sold a total of 83,000 units of this model, for 2009 the estimates are around 100,000 passenger cars, supposing an increase in sales of 17% in the European market.
As for the Spanish market, Honda has sold a total of 8,000 Jazz units since 2002, which corresponds to 6.7% of Honda’s total sales in Spain.
With regard to the new generation, those responsible for the Spanish branch are expected to sell around the 2,000-strologe of Jazz within a one-year.
Precisely the arrival of the new Jazz will allow the Honda to significantly reduce the drop in sales in Spain.
In fact, the managers of the company plan to arrive at the end of the year with a total of 22,000 units sold, i.e. 4,000 less than in 2007, with a decrease of 16%.
Despite the increase in sales, Honda increased its market share in Spain, reaching 1.8%, against 1.6% last year.
Moreover, the Japanese company expects to reach 2 percent in 2010.
In order to do so, the Honda confides in the arrival of another new model.
It’s Insight, a five-pronged and compact-format hybrid—4.57 meters in length—of which the Japanese company hopes to sell around 200,000 units a year around the world.
For the markets and according to the estimates made by the Japanese manufacturer, 50 per cent of Insight sales will take place in the U.S.
Insight’s commercial launch is scheduled for the first few months of the course of the year, soon after the company was unveiled in January in Valencia.
Compared to the first generation, the new Jazz has grown in all its parts.
Its length reaches 3.90 meters (five centimeters more), the width 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the entire 2.50 meters, with an increase of five centimeters.
Height, however, remained unchanged at 1.52 meters.
Together with the increase in size, the habitable interior also expands.
Such an advantage derives on the one hand from the insertion of a new platform, and on the other from the introduction of the fuel tank into a central position, a solution also adopted in the previous Jazz model.
In addition, the luggage capacity – equipped with a double background – rises to 399 liters, i.e. 19 liters plus first-generation capacity.
It should be noted that these are the measurements of the engine version 1.4, and the luggage capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 liters.
Other enhancements - in addition to external elements, such as rear LEDs or the possibility of mounting panoramic glass - concern an improvement in accessibility to the rear seats.
The gates that allow access to the rear positions open with an angle of 80 degrees, compared to the 67 degrees of the first Jazz.
In addition, the engines in this second generation of jazz are also new.
At the time of commercialization, this small model will be proposed in models with 1.2 and 1.4-litre gasoline engines.
Both mechanics include i-VTEC technology that reduces fuel utilisation, produces fewer carbon dioxide emissions, optimizes the mechanics and at the same time maintains high engine torque in all its phases.
The 1.2-lit-kilore model allows you to take advantage of a power of 90 hp at 6,000 rpm with a maximum torque of 114 Nm at 4,900 rpm.
Propulsion – which reached 78 horsepower in the previous Jazz – uses 5.3 liters of fuel and produces CO2 emissions equal to about 125 gram.
Compared to the previous propulsion, which in the first-to-generation model used 83 horsepower, there are now 100.
The 100-horsepower Jazz 1.4 achieves a combination of 5.4 liters of fuel and 128 grams of CO2 emitted.
"Ape car" to avoid collisions
As part of the development of accident-prevention technologies, Nissan has been inspired, this time, by nature, and especially bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot that, for example, the flight of bees, knows how to avoid accidents.
The Bio-mimimetic Car Robot Drive, or BR23C, the first step towards collision prevention technologies.
Engineers at Nissan Motor Co.’s Advanced Technical Centre developed this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Scientific and Technological Research at the prestigious University of Tokyo in Japan.
Kazuhiro Doi, Director-General of Public Relations for Technology and Research and the Development of Brand Management, the BR23C forms an extension of Nissan’s four-area Safety Shield.
“This robot is designed to improve the ability to prevent collisions in the manufacturer’s vehicles.
During flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space that is very similar to Nissan’s safety shield.
But the most important thing is bee-composite eyes, able to see in all directions at an angle greater than 300 degrees, and that allow the insect to fly without interruption in its personal space.
To re-encreate this visual functionality, the engineers developed what they called a localization laser (Laser Range Finder).
“This mechanism detects obstacles in front of the BR23C for a radius within two metres and with a maximum amplitude of 180 degrees, calculates the distance from the object and sends a signal to an on-board micro-processor that immediately prevents collision.
“At the moment it detects an obstacle, the little robot mimics the movements of a bee and immediately changes direction by turning on the wheels at a right or greater angle to avoid the collision.
“The most noticeable difference with any assisted-assisted system is the evasion manoeuvre is totally instinctive.
“Had that wasn’t, the robot wouldn’t be able to react as quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, Mobilibilità Laboratory director and chief engineer of this project.
Unlike a bee, the robot cannot deviate upwards or downwards, not even diagonally; it can move only in two dimensions and in the direction in which they can turn its reversals and does not take advantage of infinite directions and ways like bees do.
Because of this, the Nissan used a rotation function, in addition to acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot can avoid collisions.
"This device only needs to process the information every few seconds, and to act from this data.
It doesn’t require the storage of information or the intuitive logic of previous manoeuvres, so it can work without interruption and with only a couple of dozen KB of memory.
“That may be surprising, but we’re not talking gigabytes of memorabilia, or even mega.”
“It’s the first step towards the development of new technologies for instantaneous collision prevention, a technology that Nissan hopes will be standard in cars within a ten-year.
The robot uses lithium-polypolypolymer batteries, which are lighter and more durable than lithium-ion-ion type.
Another potential future application of this technology could be the integration of the units with a GPS satellite navigation system to bring the driver to the destination avoiding the obstacles present in the route.
