Morgan Stanley opens its capital to MUFG
The Morgan Stanley has announced the signing of an agreement with the Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), the first bank in Japan, that it will invest nine billion dollars in a 21% shareholding in the Company, news received following a sharp decline in its shares on the Stock Exchange.
A week after a tentaitable agreement, the two banks explained that MUFG would buy 9.9 percent of Morgan Stanley’s equi-equities at an individual cost of $ 25.25 at an individual cost of $2.55.
Excluded price is 19% lower than the book value of securities at 31 August.
MUFG will also invest US$6 billion in purchasing convertible securities at a conversion price of $31.25, ensuring a 10% return.
The Morgan Stanley has clarlarified that the global operation would be carried out on the base of an average price of 29 dollars in stock, or a percentage lower than its current course of 28%.
Around 7.5 a.m. Morgan Stanley stock fell 6.79% to $23.07.
"This strategic alliance is an important opportunity to accelerate the transition of Morgan Stanley as a bank holding company," explained John Mack, General Manager of the U.S. Group.
The Morgan Stanley adopted this new statute last week, which should allow it to differentiate its actions by making them go under the control of the Federal Reserve.
Its alliance with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis is forcing Wall Street’s biggest names to adopt a much more cautious stance than previously assumed.
The modalities defined on Monday also show that in the last few days the agreement has been modified: last Monday, Morgan had simply announced the sale of 20% of its shares to the Japanese giant for a sum of 8.5 billion dollars.
"A week ago, in Tokyo, Toshihide Mizuno, MUFG's executive director, explained: "We were thinking that our participation fee would be exclusively composed of ordinary shares.
“Decising the current situation of the world economy and the market, we have chosen to minimise risks and protect ourselves.”
On the basis of weighted average price, MUFG will obtain Morgan Stanley securities at a price lower than its 7% inrecourish.
“ And the transaction would result in an investment of at least 20 percent of equity on diluted basis.”
The agreement stipulates that MUFG will be able to convict half of its preferential securities into ordinary shares in one year if, in the course of a given period, the latter are traded more than 50% below the fixed conversion price, i.e. at about $ 47.
Morgan Stanley inquires that MUFG’s capital would allow it to seize investment opportunities, strengthen its balance sheet and increase its liquidity.
A spokeswowoman for the group, however, refused to provide clarification on the strategic projects of the two new allies.
Following MUFG’s investment, Morgan Stanley is therefore expected to establish a Tier One solvency coefficient of approximately 15%, one of the best in the industry.
Morgan has clarlarified to have "clearly" lightened its budget from the end of August.
The last few few weeks have put a strain on the group, which has seen the value of its shares halved while the cost of risk-insurrender has soared.
Cost erefluencing on Monday while remaining at a high level, at around 12%.
Last week the Goldman Sachs, the only other "survivor" among Wall Street's major investment banks after the collapse of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the take-up of Merrill Lynch, announced it had collected $$15 billion, in part at Berkshire Hathaway.
- Rafael Correa à Quito, ceundi.
Determine a republic on socialism in the 21st century.
This is the co-protinuous project of the Venezuelan Hugo Chavez, of the Bolivian Evo Morales et dell'ecuadorian Rafael Correa.
Sunday, following the example of colleagues and friends, it was the latter who gave voice to the draft of a new Constitution.
Mission carried out since, according to the official results, 64% of the electorate voted "yes" in the referendum, on a voting basis equal to 80%.
According to these results, the "no" were 28%, while 7% of the votes were zero and 0.7% blank ballots.
In order for the draft constitution to be adopted, it would have to obtain half of the votes, plus one.
On Sunday, two polls carried out on polling stations and an estimation of a non-governing organization had heralded a crushing "yes" victory, placing it between 63% et 70%.
Rafael Correa had also re-proclaimed in the night between Sunday and Monday the victory of his draft Constitution.
“Constitution triumphed gloriously,” he assured, congratulating the Ecuadorians within Guayaquil’s governorate, 280 kilometers southwest of Quito.
As always, Chavez and Morales congratulated Rafael Correa, as did his Chilean counterpart Michelle Bachelet.
The scrutiny, which took place calmly according to the electoral observation mission of the Organization of American States (OEA), was based on a text of 444 points to consolidate the "new socialism" defended by the president.
“This draft Constitution consecrates the state’s power over the economy, reimbuing it with the “planning of the development” of a country that essentially lives on the oil extraction carried out by large foreign companies and their currency.
Conceived by Correa, elected in 2006, the project also granted greater power to the head of state, who can be elected for two consecutive terms of four years, will have the possibility of dissolving the assembly.
Correa has already announced its intention to exploit this amendment and re-propose its candidacy for a re-entry in February 2009.
The Constitution also affirms the gratuitousness of health and educational services, which the president has begun to promote 13.9 million inhabitants in this country, where 50% of the population lives below the poverty line.
It also forbade the presence of foreign trouvries, which will put an end to the agreement between Quito and Washington for the utilization of an Ecuadorian base as a starting point for American anti-drug operations.
