The fox in chicken coop
In the United States, insurressiers about life and health, according to personal medicinal purchasing data, often refuse to enter into an insurance contract.
“Privacy experts fear that the data market becomes uncontrollable.
“There is a privacy program according to which the use of drug pre-prescription data in any case depends on the approval of the person concerned.
“This agenda must be in independent hands, otherwise it’s like entrusting the coop to the fox” – writes the Washington Post citing Richard Dick author of the program to follow the prescriptions of the medicines.
Insurveance companies subordinate the insurance rate and the amount due in the event of claims to the state of health of any customer and if they consider too high the risk do not take out insurance.
Some time ago the customer had to undergo a medical examination and the contract was stipulated only after the outcome of the examination.
Dick 10 years ago invented a much more economical procedure: according to the identification code of the customer you can easily get information from the pharmacies on what medicines he has bought in the last year and a half and from this the program shows what diseases he suffers and in what health conditions it is found.
For example, if it regularly acquires large amounts of cholesterol reducers this means that the level of fat in the blood is in practice inaccuriturbably high and is categorized into the higher risk category.
On the market there are various versions of the program, some also determine the risk multiplier, the insurer therefore in addition to paper work does not remain much, even if there are small difficulties.
Of the cons there is that some medicines can be usable for multiple purposes, e.g. some compounds can be used against migraines or hot flaps, but they are also effective against depression.
The latter, from an insured-compulsory point of view, counts as a risk factor, the first two does not.
According to the request and data processing, separate companies were formed and these, before obtaining the necessary information from the pharmacies, they ask for the approval of the patient.
Now the authorities oblige them to do so even if so far they have not inflicted fines for negligence.
A number of companies recently also process the result of laboratory examinations and connect them to medical consumption data in order to be able to provide a more precise picture of the state of health of patients.
More and more companies have access to the data and at the same time controlling the use will be more and more difficult.
There’s a non-privacy-by-privacy law but its validity only extends to health and insurance services.
In front of the Congress there is a bill that gives the supervisory competence authorities to companies specializing in-tasted service.
Richard Dick admits that it is not enough: for this reason he has developed a program that connects the submission of data with the approval expressed by the person concerned.
Another question is that the person concerned can not so much denoil the approval because otherwise insurers do not stipulate the contract.
Then at the end the hens are entrusted to fox clemency.
